Yes. In offensive security, there are many network protocols whose binary fields, headers, flags, lengths, options or payloads can be manipulated to investigate vulnerabilities.

An important distinction: not every protocol is entirely binary. Some are text-based, some are binary-based, and others combine both.

For vulnerability research, you should understand how each protocol is structured, how its fields are encoded, and what happens when those fields contain unexpected or malformed values.

### 1\. Network protocols with binary structures

These are important protocols to study during authorized security testing.

### A. Network and Internet protocols

| Protocol | Binary fields that can be examined              | Security research areas              |
| -------- | ----------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| IPv4     | Version, IHL, TTL, flags, fragment offset       | Fragmentation, parsing errors        |
| IPv6     | Version, traffic class, flow label, hop limit   | Extension-header handling            |
| TCP      | Flags, sequence number, window, options         | State handling, parser errors        |
| UDP      | Source port, destination port, length, checksum | Length validation, datagram handling |
| ICMP     | Type, code, checksum                            | Error-message processing             |
| ICMPv6   | Type, code, checksum                            | IPv6 control-message handling        |
| ARP      | Hardware type, protocol type, operation         | Address-resolution validation        |
| GRE      | Flags, version, protocol type                   | Encapsulation parsing                |
| ESP      | SPI, sequence number, padding                   | IPsec packet processing              |
| AH       | SPI, sequence number, authentication data       | Authentication validation            |

